{"verdict":"approved","issues":[{"severity":"minor","file":"test/schedule.test.ts","line":17,"description":"The verification criterion 'each pair of consecutive stops separated by the arrow separator ' -> '' is only covered by the pre-existing test `it('separates stops with an arrow')` which asserts `toContain('->')` — matching the substring without surrounding spaces. This does not verify the full ' -> ' separator between every consecutive pair. The code itself is correct (`parts.join(' -> ')` is unchanged), so this is a test-coverage gap, not a code defect. Per blocker discipline this is out of the implementer's scope (the test pre-exists the fix), so it is recorded as a minor note only.","suggestedFix":"Optionally strengthen the existing assertion to `expect(formatStops(LINE)).toBe('Union (0m) -> Bloor (12m) -> Eglinton (25m)')` or assert `toContain(' -> ')` with surrounding spaces."},{"severity":"minor","file":"src/schedule.ts","line":14,"description":"File does not end with a newline character (`\\ No newline at end of file` in diff). Cosmetic only; does not affect compilation or tests.","suggestedFix":"Add a trailing newline at end of file."}],"summary":"The fix for MOCK3-2 is the prescribed minimal one-character change: the loop bound in `formatStops` changed from `i < stops.length - 1` to `i < stops.length`, making the final stop appear in the rendered departure board. No other logic, constants, or APIs were altered. Two new `it` blocks were added in the existing `describe('formatStops')` block: one asserting the final stop 'Eglinton' appears in output (would have failed pre-fix), and one asserting `formatStops([])` returns '' without error. Both `tsc --noEmit` (strict: true) and `vitest run` pass with exit code 0 — 6/6 tests green. No new dependencies, imports, or secrets introduced. No public API changes. All three acceptance criteria are met: (1) final stop now renders, (2) arrow separator logic unchanged and correct, (3) empty line renders without error. The only gap is that the pre-existing arrow-separator test asserts `toContain('->')` rather than the full ` -> ` with spaces — a minor test-coverage note, not a code defect, and outside the implementer's prescribed scope."}
